-- title:
--   Theorem 6 — Douglas–Rachford splitting is the proximal point algorithm on $S_{\lambda,A,B}$
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal H$ be a real Hilbert space, $\lambda>0$, $A,B$ maximal monotone operators on $\mathcal H$, and let $J_{\lambda A},J_{\lambda B}:\mathcal H\to\mathcal H$ be their resolvents. Let
--   $$G_{\lambda,A,B}=J_{\lambda A}\circ(2J_{\lambda B}-I)+(I-J_{\lambda B}).$$
--   Then
--
--   1. for every $x\in\mathcal H$, the resolvent $(I+S_{\lambda,A,B})^{-1}$ takes the single value $G_{\lambda,A,B}(x)$ at $x$;
--   2. consequently a sequence $\{z^k\}$ satisfies the Douglas–Rachford recursion $z^{k+1}=G_{\lambda,A,B}(z^k)$ for all $k\ge0$ if and only if $z^{k+1}=(I+S_{\lambda,A,B})^{-1}(z^k)$ for all $k\ge0$, i.e. it is generated by the proximal point algorithm applied to $S_{\lambda,A,B}$ with stepsizes $c_k=1$ and exact evaluation of resolvents.
--
--   This identification is the paper's new characterization of Douglas–Rachford splitting; with Theorems 3 and 5 it yields the convergence theory of Theorem 7.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The resolvents $J_{\lambda A},J_{\lambda B}$ are maps with $\tfrac1\lambda(x-J(x))\in A(J(x))$ (resp. $B$) for all $x$, unique by Corollary 2.2. Because $S_{\lambda,A,B}$ is defined by its set formula, item 1 is a genuine statement and not a definitional unfolding.
-- source:
--   Eckstein and Bertsekas, On the Douglas–Rachford Splitting Method and the Proximal Point Algorithm for Maximal Monotone Operators, MIT LIDS-P-1919 (October 1989), p. 18, Theorem 6 (G_{λ,A,B} as on p. 16)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ThreeOpSplitting_Convergence_MonotoneOperators
import Definitions.Def_DouglasRachfordPPA_GenDR_Operators
import Definitions.Def_DouglasRachfordPPA_GenDR_SplittingOperator

open InnerProductSpace ThreeOpSplitting.Convergence

namespace DouglasRachfordPPA.GenDR
/-- Theorem 6: for `lam > 0`, maximal monotone `A`, `B` with resolvent maps
`JA = J_{lam A}`, `JB = J_{lam B}`, the Douglas–Rachford map
`G = JA ∘ (2 JB - I) + (I - JB)` is the resolvent `(I + S_{lam,A,B})⁻¹` (proximal point step
with `c_k = 1`, exact evaluation), so a sequence obeys the Douglas–Rachford recursion iff it
obeys the exact proximal point recursion for `S_{lam,A,B}` with unit stepsizes. -/
theorem douglas_rachford_is_proximal_point {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H] [CompleteSpace H]
    (lam : ℝ) (hlam : 0 < lam) (A B : H → Set H)
    (hA : IsMaximalMonotone A) (hB : IsMaximalMonotone B)
    (JA JB : H → H) (hJA : IsResolvent lam A JA) (hJB : IsResolvent lam B JB) :
    (∀ x : H, opResolvent 1 (splittingOp lam A B) x = {drMap JA JB x}) ∧
    (∀ z : ℕ → H, (∀ k, z (k + 1) = drMap JA JB (z k)) ↔
      (∀ k, z (k + 1) ∈ opResolvent 1 (splittingOp lam A B) (z k))) := by sorry

end DouglasRachfordPPA.GenDR
